    "response": " To solve this problem using Newton's Law of Cooling, we start with the differential equation that describes the cooling process:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = -k(T - T_a)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( T \\) is the temperature of the metal part at time \\( t \\), \\( T_a \\) is the ambient temperature (22°C), and \\( k \\) is the cooling constant. We can solve this differential equation to get the general solution:\n\n\\[\nT(t) = T_a + (T_0 - T_a)e^{-kt}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( T_0 \\) is the initial temperature of the metal part (180°C). Plugging in the known values, we have:\n\n\\[\nT(t) = 22 + (180 - 22)e^{-kt} = 22 + 158e^{-kt}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that after 6 minutes, the temperature of the metal part is 120°C. So, we can substitute \\( t = 6 \\) and \\( T(6) = 120 \\) into the equation to find \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\n120 = 22 + 158e^{-6k}\n\\]\n\nSubtract 22 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n98 = 158e^{-6k}\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 158:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{98}{158} = e^{-6k}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{49}{79} = e^{-6k}\n\\]\n\nTake the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\ln\\left(\\frac{49}{79}\\right) = -6k\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\n",
